# Payverge edge, EDGE=cloudflare: Cloudflare proxies DOMAIN (orange cloud)
# and connects to this Caddy, which still terminates TLS for the origin.
#
# Client IP: {client_ip} is taken from CF-Connecting-IP ONLY when the TCP peer
# is inside the checked-in Cloudflare ranges (cloudflare-cidrs.caddy,
# regenerated by scripts/update-cloudflare-cidrs.ts). A request that reaches
# the origin directly cannot choose its IP by sending that header; Caddy then
# uses the TCP peer. If Cloudflare ever omits CF-Connecting-IP, Caddy falls
# back to X-Forwarded-For read right to left (trusted_proxies_strict), so a
# client-supplied first entry is still ignored. payverge.caddy forwards
# {client_ip} to the apps as the only X-Forwarded-For value and removes
# CF-Connecting-IP and the other client-identity headers.
#
# Cloudflare settings this file expects:
#   - SSL/TLS mode "Full (strict)". Caddy still obtains a public certificate
#     for the origin; the ACME HTTP-01 challenge passes through the proxy.
#   - Optional: firewall ports 80/443 to the Cloudflare ranges if you want to
#     refuse direct-to-origin traffic. Client-IP handling is safe without it.
#
# Environment: the same as Caddyfile (DOMAIN, CADDY_ACME_EMAIL_OPTION,
# CADDY_HSTS). TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS is not used here, and TLS_TERMINATION=proxy
# is refused (docker-compose.yml), so CADDY_SITE_SCHEME is always unset.
{
	{$CADDY_ACME_EMAIL_OPTION}

	servers {
		timeouts {
			read_header 15s
			idle 2m
		}
		import cloudflare-cidrs.caddy
		trusted_proxies_strict
	}
}

import payverge.caddy
